WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.552
Once we can identify individual data-quality problems, we can summarize them.

00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:11.267
This query creates a temporary issue list using a common table expression.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:15.167
One part identifies invalid customer emails.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:18.887
Another identifies invalid customer phone numbers.

00:00:18.887 --> 00:00:22.372
Another identifies invalid product prices.

00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:26.948
The union all operation combines those issue records into one list.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:34.581
Then the final query groups by entity type and issue type and counts how many records have each problem.

00:00:34.581 --> 00:00:42.776
Instead of reading the long SQL statement character by character, focus on the pattern: detect problems first,

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:47.856
represent each problem as a row, then aggregate the issue rows.

00:00:47.856 --> 00:00:51.576
This approach is useful for dashboards and monitoring.

00:00:51.576 --> 00:00:59.356
It can answer questions such as: what is our most common quality problem, and is that problem getting better or worse

00:00:59.356 --> 00:00:59.914
over time?
